  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 219 PM CDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nThe aforementioned ridge will continue to build and slowly track east \ntowards the Great Lakes Region Monday and Tuesday. This will bring a \nperiod of quiet and dry weather through the start of the week, with \nseasonal temperatures and light winds. \n\nTuesday night and Wednesday, we will see this upper ridge break down \nwith a wave pushing southeast towards the area, flattening the \nridge. The wave will pass through the area Wednesday. Moisture \nwill be a little slow to get here, with best warm advection \nmoving into the area later in the night Tuesday. This may limit \nprecipitation chances to Wednesday, when we see the best forcing\nand moisture pooling along the approaching boundary. While \nthere are some subtle differences in timing, most global models \nfavor precipitation onset in our northwest Wednesday morning and\nthe boundary slowly working southeast through the day \nWednesday. As of right now, there is much uncertainty regarding \nthe chance for strong to severe storms. Instability will be \nmarginal, but we might have sufficient shear for some organized \nconvection Wednesday afternoon/evening. Although, confidence is \nlow. Will continue to monitor and update. \n\nAfter the midweek system passes, we will remain on the southern end \nof a broad upper trof over central North America. This pattern will \nkeep us under the path of weak shortwaves passing along the flow of \nthe upper trof. Thus, we will continue to see near-daily Slight-\nChance PoPs through late week and into the weekend, especially with \na baroclinic zone developing over the region. Guidance favors \nstronger southwest flow in the llvls under this pattern, which will \nallow for temperatures and humidity to increase during this \ntimeframe.",
